          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#regge_calculus"} Regge calculus
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#t_hooftvan_de_meent"} ‘t Hooft-van de Meent
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesCDT"} Causal Dynamical Triangulation
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            If one allows
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifolds"} pseudo-Riemannian manifolds
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+singularities"} conical singularities
            then it makes sense to ask for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            which are flat (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometric
            to pieces of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} strata
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            concentrated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+singularities"} conical singularities
            on these lower-dimensional strata.
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ConicalSingularity.jpg", :width => "300"}/
          %p
            Such piecewise flat spacetimes have been considered as discretized models for smooth (non-singular and non-piecewise flat)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            useful for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            (see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#WilliamsTuckey92"} Williams &amp; Tuckey 1992
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional"} functional
            on piecewise flat spacetimes, depending on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edge"} edge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lengths"} lengths
            of a metric
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complex
            %a{:href => "#Regge61"} Regge 1961
            (see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Barrett87"} Barrett 1987
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            it approaches the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional"} functional
            on non-singular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            = succeed "." do
              %em Regge calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            indeed works out has been proven (only) in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CheegerMuellerSchrader84"} Cheeger, Mueller &amp;Schrader 1984
            %a{:href => "#Cheeger16"} Cheeger 2016
            for review.
          %p
            A variant of this perspective, but with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+singularities"} conical singularities
            constrained to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/timelike"} timelike
            as expected for “physical” singularities, has been initiated in
            %a{:href => "#tHooft08"} ‘t Hooft 2008
            and worked out in some detail by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#vandeMeent11"} van de Meent 2011
          %p
            In both cases a more speculative motivation for considering piecewise flat spacetimes is the hope that it might help with defining
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+QFT"} non-perturbatively
            (
            %a{:href => "#ReggeWilliams00"}> Regge &amp; Williams 2000
            ). A direct attempt to define and compute a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            over piecewise flat spacetimes is known as “causal dynamical triangulation” (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AmbjornJurkiewiczLoll00"} Ambjorn, Jurkiewicz &amp;Loll 20000
          %p
            Piecewise flat spacetimes appear naturally in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3-dimensional gravity
          %p
            But piecewise flat spacetimes also appear naturally as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/far-horizon+geometry"}> far-horizon geometry
            ” (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+1%2FN+limit"}>
              small
              = succeed "-limit" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            ”, see there) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+state"} BPS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
            spacetimes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories, where considerations such as discussed at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            suggest that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+singularities"} conical singularities
            have to be taken seriously as part of the physical model. These
            = succeed "-singularities" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone+brane"} cone brane
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#tHooft08"} ‘t Hooft 2008
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#vandeMeent11"} van de Meent 2011
